WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.867
Consider the word bank in two sentences: the customer went to the bank to deposit a check,

00:00:05.867 --> 00:00:08.592
and the child sat near the river bank.

00:00:08.592 --> 00:00:15.982
A static embedding method such as Word2Vec learns one general vector for the vocabulary item bank.

00:00:15.982 --> 00:00:21.872
That vector reflects many training contexts, but it does not become a different vector simply because the

00:00:21.872 --> 00:00:25.207
current sentence is about finance or geography.

00:00:25.207 --> 00:00:28.952
A Transformer starts with an initial token embedding,

00:00:28.952 --> 00:00:32.977
then repeatedly updates that representation through attention.

00:00:32.977 --> 00:00:39.502
In the financial sentence, words such as deposit and check can influence the representation.

00:00:39.502 --> 00:00:45.447
In the geographic sentence, river and nearby context can influence it differently.

00:00:45.447 --> 00:00:52.282
After several Transformer layers, the two occurrences of bank can have different contextual vectors.

00:00:52.282 --> 00:00:58.132
This is one of the most important changes from static embeddings to contextual language models:

00:00:58.132 --> 00:01:01.724
meaning is represented together with the surrounding context.
